{
 "id": "l20",
 "label": "20",
 "title": "Magkaroón: to have",
 "summary": "Magkaroón ng + noun and the short magka- + noun, in the past, present and future",
 "minutes": 35,
 "goals": [
  "Use magkaroón ng + noun to say someone has something",
  "Shorten it to magka- + noun",
  "Form the past, present and future",
  "Place the subject correctly after magkaroón",
  "Make negative sentences with hindî"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "What magkaroón means",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The word **magkaroón** means *to have*. It is usually placed **before a noun** to say someone has what the noun expresses."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Magkaroón may be shortened to **magka-**. It is similar in meaning to **may** and **mayroón** (Lesson 12), which also express existence and/or actual possession."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "rule",
     "title": "The big difference",
     "items": [
      "**Magkaroón** has tense forms: past, present and future.",
      "**May** and **mayroón** do not have any tense forms, except when may is followed by a verb."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "pattern",
     "title": "Two ways to say to have",
     "rows": [
      {
       "label": "Long",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "magkaroón",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "ng",
         "r": "marker"
        },
        {
         "t": "pera",
         "r": "noun"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "Short",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "magka-",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "pera",
         "r": "noun"
        },
        {
         "t": "→ magkapera",
         "r": "pred"
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "note": "magkaroón + article ng + noun, or magka- + noun: to have money"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Forming phrases and words with magkaroón",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Follow either formula: **magkaroón + ng + noun**, or **magka- + noun**. Both mean the same thing."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Tense",
      "Long form",
      "Short form",
      "How it is made",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Infinitive",
       "[[magkaroón|pred]] [[ng|marker]] [[pera|noun]]",
       "[[magkapera|pred]]",
       "magka- + noun",
       "to have money"
      ],
      [
       "Past",
       "[[nagkaroón|pred]] [[ng|marker]] [[pera|noun]]",
       "[[nagkapera|pred]]",
       "magkaroón becomes nagkaroón; nagka + noun",
       "had money"
      ],
      [
       "Present",
       "[[nagkakaroón|pred]] [[ng|marker]] [[pera|noun]]",
       "[[nagkákapera|pred]]",
       "magkaroón becomes nagkakaroón; nagkaka + noun",
       "has money"
      ],
      [
       "Future",
       "[[magkakaroón|pred]] [[ng|marker]] [[pera|noun]]",
       "[[magkakapera|pred]]",
       "magkaroón becomes magkakaroón; magkaka + noun",
       "will have money"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Magkaroón with pera",
     "note": "Accents as printed in the book's table. In the sample sentences the book writes the present and future as nagkákaroón and magkákaroón."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Spot the tense",
     "items": [
      "**m** → **n** for the past and present: **n**agkaroón, **n**agkakaroón.",
      "The present and future repeat **ka**: nag**ka**karoón, mag**ka**karoón.",
      "The short forms follow the same pattern: nagkapera, nagkákapera, magkakapera."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "magkaroon",
     "title": "The forms of magkaroón"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Where the subject goes",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The subject of a sentence using magkaroón or a magka- word is introduced by **si** or **ang**, or is shown by a **nominative pronoun** such as **siyá**."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "rule",
     "title": "Subject position",
     "items": [
      "A **pronoun** subject is **always placed after magkaroón**: Nagkaroón **siyá** ng anák na kambál.",
      "A subject introduced by **si** or **ang** may go **after magkaroón** or **after the object**.",
      "The meaning does not change."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Nagkaroón si Maria ng asó.",
       "en": "Had Maria a dog. (literal) Maria had a dog.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Nagkaroón",
         "had",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "si",
